🧠 Study notes
01 / DevOps starts with shared responsibility
DevOps brings development and operations practices together to improve how software is built, tested, released, and operated. Collaboration, repeatable automation, and feedback help teams deliver changes reliably.
My takeaway: Writing working code is one part of the job. Delivering it consistently and understanding its behaviour after release matter too.

A familiar example: My Express API works on my laptop but fails on a server because an environment variable is missing. Documented configuration, repeatable setup, and deployment checks can help prevent this problem.
02 / Agile and DevOps work together
Approach	Main emphasis
Waterfall	Work organised into sequential phases
Agile	Small iterations and frequent feedback
DevOps	Collaboration throughout software delivery and operation


03 / CI and CD are different parts of the workflow
Practice	What it does	Production release
Continuous integration	Integrates small changes frequently and checks them with automated builds and tests	Not required
Continuous delivery	Keeps validated changes ready for release through a repeatable process	A human release decision can remain
Continuous deployment	Releases qualifying changes after all required checks pass	Automatic


Remember: Pushing to GitHub alone is not CI. A green pipeline means its configured checks passed; it does not guarantee bug-free software.
04 / Builds produce artifacts
An artifact is an output that can be stored, tested, or deployed—for example, a React dist folder, a Java JAR, or a container image. Associating an artifact with a source commit helps identify exactly what was released.
<details>
<summary><strong>🔎 Example — a delivery workflow for my booking application</strong></summary>

1. Retrieve the proposed source revision.
2. Install dependencies using the lockfile.
3. Run configured lint checks and automated tests.
4. Build and label the artifact.
5. Deploy it to staging and check the booking flow.
6. Approve the production release.
7. Deploy, check health, and monitor errors.
This is a workflow design exercise; implementation is a future lab. Required test failures should block promotion. Database changes also need a migration and recovery plan.
</details>

